Original commit body from 3468ba3e182eb5002c0cde81715d220ea665a45c:

8.1.0's CHANGELOG said "Released **schema** is at: vdid not say why".
prepare-release read SCHEMA_VERSION with sed 's/.*"\(.*\)".*/\1/', which
keeps the LAST quoted string on the line; schema.py's SCHEMA_VERSION line
carries a long comment whose last quoted phrase is "did not say why".

- All 16 version reads in bump-version, release, prepare-release,
  tag-release and release-check now take the FIRST quoted string. The
  pyproject reads happened to be safe (one quoted string per line); they
  change so the next trailing comment cannot break them.
- prepare-release stops if the schema value is not MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.

The Version History table at the bottom of CHANGELOG.md stopped at 2.1.0
(eighteen releases missing) and its link definitions pointed at
Codeberg. Nothing maintained either.

- Rows added for 2.2.0 through 8.1.0, taken from each section's summary
  and the release-notes TL;DRs; 2.5.1's empty section is described from
  its one commit (security pins).
- Link definitions regenerated from the real tags against GitHub, each
  comparing the previous TAG (so 1.2.1 compares v1.2.0, which has no
  heading). Versions with no tag (2.0.1, 1.3.x, 1.0.0, 0.5.0) get no
  link; the old block linked a v0.5.0 tag that does not exist.
- prepare-release promotes a "| Unreleased | ... |" row to the version
  and stops if the table has no row for it, the same gate the release
  notes have. It writes the [VERSION] compare link and moves
  [Unreleased] itself, since those are derived.

tests/test_prepare_release_version_history.py executes the shipped blocks:
the schema read against the real schema.py (equal to the imported
constant) and against the 8.1.0 shape, the guard, promotion, refusal and
idempotence, plus the live CHANGELOG's two version lines and full table
coverage. 8 of 9 fail against the pre-fix script and CHANGELOG; all pass
now. The manifest's 2,287 tests pass.

